 for bestpractices and # Cronjob Detail

## Overview
Create the Cronjob Detail page and its surrounding architecture as part of LifeOps’ Master Dashboard. This page provides a detailed, editable view of a single cronjob as a first-class object, encapsulating schedule configuration, trigger mapping, input payload templating, constraints, safety rails, retry policy, and outputs. It must integrate with RBAC for granular permissions, a Conflict Resolution & Policy Engine to justify decisions and resolve conflicts, and the Cronjobs Engine (the central object model for scheduling and execution). The UI is dark-mode-first, modular, card-based, and accessible. All data structures are validated, auditable, and reversible, with full run artifacts and inter-agent traces available in the Run Detail view.

## Page Description (Full Detail)
What this page is:
- A single-cronjob Detailed View + Editor within the LifeOps Master Dashboard. It presents a complete, editable representation of a cronjob as a first-class object, including:
  - Schedule builder: cron expression, UI-assisted builder, timezone, and next/last run previews.
  - Trigger config: type (time, event, conditional), mappings to events or agents, and fallbacks.
  - Input payload templating: prompt template editor with variables, scope, and runtime variable resolution; live preview of inputs fed to the target agent/workflow.
  - Constraints: max actions per run, spend limits, allowed tools, and guardrails against unsafe operations.
  - Safety rails: required confirmations, multi-step approvals, and reversible actions.
  - Retry policy: backoff strategy, max retries, and dead-letter sink configuration.
  - Outputs: per-run outputs including run history, logs, inter-agent trace, diffs, artifacts, errors, and revert actions if reversible.
  - Permissions and automation level controls: granular settings for who can configure, approve, or auto-execute cronjobs, and agent-level permissioning.
  - Human-readable justifications from Conflict Resolution & Policy Engine for any constraints or precedence decisions affecting this cronjob.
  - Audit trail: full event history, who made changes, and rollback actions.

Connected features & pages:
- RBAC: Determine who can view, edit, approve, or enable/disable the cronjob; supports workspace-scoped roles and agent-level permissions.
- Conflict Resolution & Policy Engine: On edits or schedule changes, present a human-readable justification and a resolved state, with the ability to override via approvals when policies permit.
- Cronjobs Engine: The cronjob object is the source of truth for scheduling; schedule changes propagate to the engine, with resulting runs and outputs captured.

UI elements and visual guidance:
- Header: Cronjob name, enabled/paused toggle, owner, last run summary, next run preview, quick actions (Save, Approve, Re-run, Revert).
- Schedule Builder: Interactive cron expression builder, time zone selector, and a preview panel showing upcoming runs; validation messages for invalid expressions.
- Trigger Config: Selection control (Time, Event, Conditional) with dynamic forms for each type; mapping fields to event sources; ability to test trigger mapping.
- Input Payload Editor: Rich text or structured JSON/YAML editor with syntax highlighting, variables palette, scope selector, and a live preview of the rendered payload.
- Permissions & Automation Level: Segmented controls or select menus to choose roles (e.g., Suggest-Only, Approval-Required, Conditional Auto-Execute, Bounded Autopilot) and agent-level permission toggles.
- Constraints Panel: Cards showing max actions, spend limits, and allowed tools with inline validators.
- Safety Rails: Confirmation steps with checkboxes or prompts; ability to configure required confirmations for critical actions.
- Retry Policy Editor: Controls for backoff strategy (e.g., exponential, fixed), backoff intervals, max retries, and dead-letter sink configuration with validation.
- Outputs Panel: Collapsible sections for Run History, each run’s details (inputs, outputs, traces, logs, artifacts, errors, diffs), and per-run revert actions.
- Inter-Agent Trace: Visual trace viewer showing agent-to-agent handoffs with an interactive chain diagram.
- Run Detail Modal/Page: When selecting a run, navigate to Run Detail with full context (see separate Run Detail page).

Visual style and interactions:
- Cards with rounded corners (12–16px), soft shadows, thin borders; hover states emphasize elevation.
- Sidebar navigation (if present) uses icons + labels with active state highlighting via accent color.
- Tables and lists use the 12-column grid for consistent spacing; typography follows the project’s weights and tracking.
- Data visualizations (for run histories or traces) use neon accents on a dark background; charts are non-intrusive with clear axes and legends.

API integrations:
- No external APIs for this page beyond internal LifeOps services. All data should flow through the app’s internal API layer, honoring existing authentication/authorization.

## Components to Build
- CronjobDetailPage
  - Contains all sub-sections as modular components.
- ScheduleBuilder
  - UI cron builder with cron expression, timezone, and next-run previews.
- TriggerConfigEditor
  - Dynamic form for Time/Event/Conditional triggers; includes mapping UI and tester.
- PayloadTemplateEditor
  - Editor for prompt templates with variable insertion playground and scope management; live input preview.
- ConstraintsPanel
  - Cards for max actions, spend limits, allowed tools; validation and quick-add suggestions.
- SafetyRailsEditor
  - Confirmation steps, required prompts, and reversible actions configuration.
- RetryPolicyEditor
  - Backoff strategies, max retries, dead-letter sink settings.
- PermissionsPanel
  - RBAC controls and automation level selectors; agent-level permission toggles.
- OutputsPanel
  - Run history list with per-run expandable details; links to Run Detail.
- RunTraceViewer
  - Inter-agent trace visualization with clickable nodes and timing data.
- RunDetailPanel (Modal/Drawer or separate route)
  - Full per-run detail with inputs, outputs, logs, diffs, artifacts, errors, and revert actions.
- AuditTrailViewer
  - Display of change history and revert actions.

## Implementation Requirements

### Frontend
- Framework: React 18+ with TypeScript.
- State management: Use React hooks and context where appropriate; lift state to a dedicated CronjobDetailContainer for data flow.
- Data fetching: Centralized API client with safe guards.
- Runtime safety:
  - All arrays derived from API responses must be guarded:
    - const items = data ?? [] ;
    - Array.isArray(items) ? items.map(...) : []
  - Initialize useState with correct types:
    - const [payload, setPayload] = useState<PayloadTemplate[]>([]);
  - Validate API responses before usage:
    - const runs = Array.isArray(response?.data?.runs) ? response.data.runs : [];
  - Optional chaining for nested API objects.
  - Destructuring with defaults:
    - const { items = [], count = 0 } = response ?? {};
- UI components:
  - Reusable Card components with consistent padding, borders, and hover states.
  - Form controls with accessible labels, proper aria attributes, and keyboard navigation.
  - Editor components: lightweight code editor (syntax-highlighted) for payload templates; ensure safe fallback if editor fails.
- Validation:
  - Real-time validation on cron expressions, trigger mappings, budget/spend values, and retry backoff parameters.
  - Inline error messages and disabled states for actions when invalid.
- Accessibility:
  - High contrast text, focus rings in accent color, and screen-reader friendly labels.
- Testing:
  - Unit tests for utility functions (e.g., cron expression validation).
  - Tests ensuring nullish data handling paths are safe (mock null responses to confirm no crashes).
- Performance:
  - Debounced save for templates, minimal re-renders when editing schedules, and memoized components where appropriate.

### Backend
- Endpoints (to be consumed by the frontend; implement as needed or extend existing services):
  - GET /workspaces/{wsId}/cronjobs/{cronId} → Cronjob detail payload
  - PATCH /workspaces/{wsId}/cronjobs/{cronId} → Partial updates to cronjob
  - POST /workspaces/{wsId}/cronjobs/{cronId}/trigger-test → Validate trigger config with a test payload
  - POST /workspaces/{wsId}/cronjobs/{cronId}/run → Manually enqueue a run
  - GET /workspaces/{wsId}/cronjobs/{cronId}/runs → Run history with details
  - GET /workspaces/{wsId}/cronjobs/{cronId}/runs/{runId} → Run Detail
  - GET /workspaces/{wsId}/policies/precedence → Policy engine precedence rules
  - POST /workspaces/{wsId}/policies/evaluate → Evaluate current cronjob against policies to generate justifications
- Database schema considerations:
  - cronjobs: id, name, enabled, schedule_cron, timezone, trigger_type, trigger_config JSON, input_template JSON, scope JSON, permissions JSON, constraints JSON, safety_rails JSON, retry_policy JSON, outputs JSON, owner_id, last_run_id, next_run_at, created_at, updated_at
  - runs: id, cronjob_id, started_at, finished_at, status, inputs JSON, outputs JSON, trace JSON, diffs JSON, artifacts JSON, errors JSON
  - policies/audit: policy_id, cronjob_id, reason, justified_by, applied_at, new_state
- Validation:
  - All update mutations must validate user permissions via RBAC.
  - Ensure null-safe handling of nested JSON fields.

### Integration
- Data flow:
  - CronjobDetailPage fetches cronjob data on mount; subscribes to updates via WebSocket or polling for run updates.
  - ScheduleBuilder and TriggerConfigEditor emit changes; changes persist to backend via PATCH with a diff-safe payload.
  - Payload editor variables and scope resolve to the target agent/workflow input schema.
  - Conflict Resolution Engine runs on change and on save to compute justification and potential overrides; display results in a non-blocking panel with a “Resolve” action if allowed.
  - RBAC checks gate all UI actions; Approvals queue surfaces actions requiring review.
  - Run History and Run Detail navigate to RunDetailPage; inter-agent trace loads from run.trace field.
  - Outputs panel pulls per-run details and artifacts from run outputs.
- Security:
  - All API calls guarded by authentication tokens; enforce authorization at API layer.
  - Validate all input payloads server-side; sanitize templates to prevent code injection in previews.

## User Experience Flow
1. User navigates to Cronjob Detail from Cronjobs Dashboard.
2. Page loads with cronjob metadata: name, owner, status, next run, last run summary.
3. User uses Schedule Builder to adjust cron expression, adjusts timezone, and previews next runs.
4. User selects Trigger Type and configures mappings; tests trigger mapping with a tester.
5. User edits Input Payload Template; uses variables palette to insert placeholders; views live preview of rendered inputs.
6. User reviews and adjusts Constraints (max actions, spend limits, allowed tools) with inline validation.
7. User configures Safety Rails (confirmations required) and Retry Policy (backoff, max retries, dead-letter).
8. User adjusts Permissions & Automation Level; RBAC rules are applied; if approvals are required, the Approvals queue is updated.
9. Conflict Resolution Engine runs in background and presents human-readable justifications for the current configuration; user can accept or override within policy allowances.
10. User saves changes; if policy requires approvals, changes await approval; otherwise cronjob is updated and engine reflects new state.
11. User can run a manual run or view run history; clicking a run navigates to Run Detail for inputs, outputs, traces, logs, diffs, artifacts, errors, and revert actions if available.
12. All actions are audited; revert options are available if reversibility is configured.

## Technical Specifications

Data Models: [Schema details]
- Cronjob
  - id: string
  - workspace_id: string
  - name: string
  - enabled: boolean
  - schedule_cron: string
  - timezone: string
  - trigger_type: 'time' | 'event' | 'conditional'
  - trigger_config: object
  - input_template: object
  - scope: object
  - permissions: object
  - constraints: object
  - safety_rails: object
  - retry_policy: object
  - outputs: object
  - owner_id: string
  - last_run_id: string | null
  - next_run_at: string | null
  - created_at: string
  - updated_at: string

- Run
  - id: string
  - cronjob_id: string
  - started_at: string
  - finished_at: string
  - status: 'pending' | 'running' | 'success' | 'failed' | 'canceled'
  - inputs: object
  - outputs: object
  - trace: object
  - diffs: object
  - artifacts: object
  - errors: object

API Endpoints: [Routes and methods]
- GET /workspaces/{wsId}/cronjobs/{cronId}
- PATCH /workspaces/{wsId}/cronjobs/{cronId}
- POST /workspaces/{wsId}/cronjobs/{cronId}/trigger-test
- POST /workspaces/{wsId}/cronjobs/{cronId}/run
- GET /workspaces/{wsId}/cronjobs/{cronId}/runs
- GET /workspaces/{wsId}/cronjobs/{cronId}/runs/{runId}
- GET /workspaces/{wsId}/policies/precedence
- POST /workspaces/{wsId}/policies/evaluate

Security: 
- Authentication: OAuth2/JWT per LifeOps standard.
- Authorization: RBAC with workspace-scoped roles and agent-level permissions; enforce in API layer and UI.
- Validation: Server-side validation for all mutation requests; sanitize inputs; never trust client data.

Validation: 
- All API inputs validated:
  - Cron expressions validated against Cron expression library; if invalid, return 400 with details.
  - Trigger mappings validated per type.
  - Templates validated for required variables and scope.
  - Spend, actions, and tool lists validated for allowed values and types.

Acceptance Criteria
- [ ] The Cronjob Detail page renders with all sections (Schedule, Trigger, Payload, Constraints, Safety Rails, Retry, Outputs, Permissions) and supports read/write operations consistent with RBAC.
- [ ] Schedule Builder validates cron expressions and shows live next-run previews; edits persist via PATCH and reflect in the Cronjobs Engine.
- [ ] Trigger Config supports Time/Event/Conditional with dynamic UI and test capability; invalid mappings are rejected with helpful messages.
- [ ] Input Payload Editor supports variables, scope, and live rendered previews; saves in a safe JSON structure; null-safe data handling is enforced.
- [ ] Constraints, Safety Rails, and Retry Policy editors include real-time validation; back-end stores valid configurations only.
- [ ] RBAC controls restrict access per role; UI reflects permissions; sensitive actions require approvals when configured.
- [ ] Conflict Resolution Engine provides human-readable justifications for current config; ability to override only when policy allows.
- [ ] Run History and Run Detail display complete inputs, outputs, traces, logs, artifacts, diffs, errors, and revert actions; all data accessed with null safety guards.
- [ ] All arrays and API results guarded with data ?? [] patterns and Array.isArray checks; all useState initializations for arrays.
- [ ] No API calls crash due to missing fields; components degrade gracefully with empty states.

UI/UX Guidelines
Apply the project’s design system:
- Dark mode-first, high-contrast typography, and modular cards.
- 12-column grid with 24–32px gutters; left-aligned content; vertical navigation with active state highlighting.
- Card-based sections with clear hierarchy: bold titles, muted descriptions, actions anchored at bottom.
- Interactive elements: Primary actions in blue (#4F8CFF); secondary in gray; hover/focus states show subtle glow or outline.
- Visualizations: Neon accents for traces and run charts on dark background.

Visual Style

Color Palette:
- Primary background: #18191C; content cards: #232429, #1F2124
- Accent: #4F8CFF (blue), #5ED36D (green), #FFD66C (yellow)
- Text: #FFFFFF (primary), #AEB2B8 (secondary), #74777D (tertiary)
- Borders: #26282C / #232429 with 30–60% opacity
- No gradients; flat surfaces with depth via shadows

Typography:
- Font: Inter or similar; weights 700 (bold), 600 (semibold), 400 (regular)

Layout:
- 12-column grid; 24–32px gutters; card-based sections
- Left-aligned content; centered navigation elements

Design Philosophy
- Modern, minimalist SaaS UI; accessibility-minded; modular and scalable; strong auditability and reversibility.

Mandatory Coding Standards — Runtime Safety (CRITICAL)
- Supabase query results: Use nullish coalescing, e.g., const items = data ?? [].
- Array methods: Guard calls, e.g., (items ?? []).map(...) or Array.isArray(items) ? items.map(...) : [].
- useState for arrays/objects: Initialize with type, e.g., useState<Type[]>([]).
- API response shapes: Validate, e.g., const list = Array.isArray(response?.data) ? response.data : [].
- Optional chaining: Use obj?.property?.nested when accessing nested API responses.
- Destructuring with defaults: const { items = [], count = 0 } = response ?? {};

Connected Pages:
- Cronjobs Dashboard
- Run Detail

This prompt provides a comprehensive specification for AI development tooling to implement the Cronjob Detail feature set with all required security, integration, UI, and data validation constraints. The implementation should be split into well-structured components, each with clear props, state, and side effects, and should adhere to the runtime safety rules described above. for style.

CRITICAL DESIGN REQUIREMENTS:
- You MUST follow ALL design principles and patterns specified in the Design Reference above
- Do NOT deviate from the design specifications without explicit user permission
- Implement ALL visual design elements (colors, typography, spacing, shadows, animations) as specified
- Follow the layout patterns and component designs exactly as documented
- Ensure all interactions and micro-animations match the reference specifications

CRITICAL TECHNICAL REQUIREMENTS:
- React 18.3.1, React Router 6.30.1
- Tailwind CSS v3 with tailwind.config.js and custom keyframes
- @tailwindcss/typography plugin for rich text content
- Tailwind CSS animations (NOT Motion library or framer-motion)
- Sonner for toasts
- Recharts for data visualization
- Native fetch() with API utilities in src/lib/api.ts
- Design system with CSS custom properties
- Use RGB color values for theming
- When Supabase is configured: use Edge Functions for LLM and server-only logic; never expose API keys in the client

